analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment.

We have method add(int index, E obj). The comment:

// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every index i from 0 to index-1, the element at i either remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was null before.

We need to refer to initial size before addition; use \old(this.size()) perhaps. Also need to refer to element at i before and after. Use this.get(i) after addition, and \old(this.get(i)) before. The condition: if index between 0 and initial size inclusive. So premise: 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()). Then for all i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null)). Actually "either remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was null before" means: if old element was null, then new element also null; if old element non-null, then it stays same. That's same as equality (including null). So we can simply assert equality: this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)). In Java, equality for objects use .equals, but need to handle null. Use \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Simpler: (this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i))) || (this.get(i) != null && this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) but that's messy. In specification language, we can use \old(this.get(i)).equals(this.get(i))? But null case fails.

Maybe we can write: this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)) || (this.get(i) != null && this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But that's tautology? Actually equality of objects: either same reference or equal. But null case: null == null true. So we can simply assert this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)) || (this.get(i) != null && this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))). However if both non-null but not equal, fails.

Simplify: (this.get(i) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

We can use conditional operator? Not typical in spec but okay.

Better: (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Thus overall assert:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

Make sure parentheses. Use => lower priority than &&, so need parentheses around premise.

Thus final code:

<code>assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>

Check syntax: need semicolon at end inside code block. Good.

